     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I can't verify the protocol or the pharmacist review, and I don't know of any accepted protocol that uses 5,100 mg/day of metformin. That is about twice the labeled maximum, and a single 1,700 mg dose is also well above the usual 850 to 1,000 mg per dose. The risk is serious: lactic acidosis, and acute illness or contrast exposure during admission can make it worse.\n\nYou have a few options:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can do this right away, for example 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day).\n- **Go through your hospital's override pathway.** This would be direct entry by the prescriber or a pharmacist-verified non-standard order, with the protocol documented. That gives a verifiable record that I can't provide.\n- **Check the dose against the protocol.** If the protocol says 1,700 mg/day or 850 mg three times daily, I can enter that.\n\nLet me know which you'd like."
      }
     ],
